          <a href="#checking-yours" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Checking Your Machine</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A shop vac running without a filter behaves perfectly well. Suction is strong, the tank fills, nothing goes wrong that you can see. The damage is entirely invisible — dust eroding the impeller, packing the motor windings, and leaving the exhaust at head height — which is precisely why so many machines are run this way.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running a shop vac without a filter is safe and correct in exactly one situation — wet pickup — and damaging in every other. For dry debris, the filter is not an accessory that improves performance; it is the only thing standing between fine dust and the motor windings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The confusion is understandable, because the machine appears to work fine without one. Suction is strong, debris goes into the tank, nothing obvious goes wrong. What you cannot see is the fine fraction passing through the impeller and into the motor housing, and the same fine fraction being blown back into the room through the exhaust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both consequences arrive later. The motor damage accumulates over months. The dust exposure is invisible and, for anything mineral, genuinely matters. Neither announces itself while you are using the machine, which is exactly why the habit is so common.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The One-Line Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Filter out for water. Filter in — plus a bag — for everything dry.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The foam sleeve some models use for wet pickup is a splash barrier, not a filter, and that is fine because water pickup creates no airborne dust. Dry work without filtration damages the machine and puts the finest particles back into the air you are standing in.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="what-happens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Happens Inside the Machine
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shop vac pulls air through the tank, past the filter, through the impeller and out through the exhaust. The filter sits between the debris and everything mechanical.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dust Reaches the Impeller</h3>
          <p>
            Fine grit passing the impeller gradually erodes the blade edges. The effect is slow and cumulative: a machine that has run filterless for a few years moves noticeably less air than an identical one that has not, even with a clean filter fitted.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dust Reaches the Motor</h3>
          <p>
            This is the expensive part. Fine dust settles on the bearings and packs into the windings, where it acts as an insulator and raises operating temperature. Heat kills motors. The characteristic result is a vac that develops a dry rumble or a rising whine and then fails, usually well before its time.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dust Comes Back Out</h3>
          <p>
            Everything that passes the impeller leaves through the exhaust port at speed, which puts the finest particles — the ones that stay airborne longest and penetrate deepest into the lungs — into the room at head height. The visible dust ends up in the tank; the dangerous fraction ends up in the air.
          </p>
          <p>
            Drywall, plaster, concrete and tile dust do all three of these things quickly, because the particle size is small enough that a standard filter struggles with it even when fitted. Wood chips and leaves do them slowly, which is why a vac used only for coarse garden debris can survive years of abuse.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="the-exception" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Exception: Wet Pickup
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet pickup is the one job where removing the pleated cartridge is not merely allowed but required.
          </p>

          <p>
            A paper or pleated filter soaks in seconds, the media collapses, and the cartridge is finished. While it is wet it also blocks airflow almost completely, so the machine pulls poorly and people conclude their vac is weak when they have simply installed a wet paper barrier in the air path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water pickup creates no airborne dust, so there is nothing for a filter to capture. What the machine needs instead is protection from splashing, which is what the foam sleeve on many models provides — it keeps droplets and wet debris out of the motor housing without restricting airflow meaningfully.
          </p>

          <p>
            The float shut-off does the rest of the safety work, sealing the intake when the tank fills so water cannot reach the motor. Check it moves freely before any wet job; a float stuck on debris is a safety device that is no longer working. Our full <a href="/how-to-vacuum-water-with-a-shop-vac/">wet pickup guide</a> covers the whole routine.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="when-people-do-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why People Run Filterless Anyway
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reasons are always practical, and each has a better answer.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Reason</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Better Answer</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">The filter clogged mid-job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tap it clean; fit a collection bag next time</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Picking up large debris only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leave the filter in — it costs nothing on coarse material</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wanted more suction</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A clean filter costs little airflow; a blinded one costs half</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Filter was wet from a previous job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dry it fully, or keep a spare cartridge on the shelf</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lost or damaged the filter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replacements are $12-$35 and universally available</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The middle row is the one worth dwelling on, because it is the most common justification. It is true that a clean filter costs a small amount of airflow — but a filter caked with fine dust costs far more, and the answer to that is a bag, not an empty machine. Running filterless to chase suction trades a few percent of airflow today for motor life and air quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 'large debris only' case is also less clean than it sounds. Leaves carry fine soil, offcuts carry sawdust, and a tank of gravel is full of stone dust. There is almost no real-world debris that is genuinely free of a fine fraction.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="if-you-have-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. If You Genuinely Have to Run Without One
          </h2>

          <p>
            Occasionally there is no filter available and the job cannot wait — a pile of leaves, a spill of packing material, a tank full of offcuts. If you do it, limit the damage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work outdoors or in a space with the door open, so the exhaust is not putting fine material back into an enclosed room. Point the exhaust port away from the work and away from anyone present.
          </p>

          <p>
            Restrict it to genuinely coarse, dry debris. Never do it with anything powdery: drywall, plaster, concrete, ash, sawdust from a sander, soil or flour-fine material of any kind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the session short, and run the machine for a minute afterwards with a clean filter fitted to help clear residual dust from the air path. Then fit a proper filter and treat the whole thing as a one-off rather than a working method.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the reason you have no filter is that the last one blinded and you binned it, buy two replacements rather than one. A spare cartridge on the shelf is the single change that stops this becoming a habit, because the moment you need a clean filter is always mid-job.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="bags-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can a Bag Replace the Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, although the question is a reasonable one, because a bag does capture most of the debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            A collection bag is a coarse pre-filter. It catches the bulk before it reaches the cartridge, which is exactly why bags extend filter life so dramatically, but the media is not fine enough to stop the smallest particles. Those pass through the bag and are then caught by the cartridge — if there is one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running a bag with no cartridge means the fine fraction goes straight through to the impeller and out of the exhaust, which is most of the harm of running with no filtration at all. The two components do different jobs and they belong together.
          </p>

          <p>
            The right combination for dusty work is a bag plus the finest cartridge your machine accepts: a fine dust cartridge for wood and household dust, a HEPA-rated one for anything mineral. That arrangement keeps airflow high through a whole job, protects the motor, and captures the particles that matter. Our <a href="/shop-vac-filters-explained/">filter guide</a> sets out which combination suits which job.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One more consideration that applies to households rather than workshops: anyone in the building with asthma or a dust allergy has a direct stake in this. A filterless shop vac run indoors is, from their point of view, a machine that collects the heavy debris and redistributes the fine allergens — the fraction that actually triggers symptoms — into the air of whichever room you just cleaned. A bag and a fine cartridge cost a few dollars and remove that entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to anything you do not want inhaled by anyone: lead paint dust in an older house, rodent droppings in a loft, mouse-nest debris in a shed. These are jobs where a HEPA cartridge and a sealed bag are the minimum, and where running bare is not merely bad for the machine but genuinely the wrong approach to the task.
          </p>

        <div id="checking-yours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Checking What Your Machine Has
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shop vacs vary in how their filtration is arranged, and it is worth knowing yours specifically rather than generally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lift the motor head off and look at the cage that sits below it. A pleated cartridge slides over that cage and seals against a gasket at the top — if the gasket is missing, hardened or torn, dust bypasses the filter entirely even when one is fitted, which produces all the symptoms of running filterless while appearing perfectly normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check whether your model takes a foam sleeve for wet work, or whether it simply runs bare. Both designs exist and both are correct for their machine; what matters is that you are not fitting a foam sleeve expecting it to filter dry dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look up the fine dust and HEPA cartridge part numbers for your model and write them somewhere you will find them. Filters are model-specific, universal ones frequently seal imperfectly, and the moment you need one is rarely a moment you want to spend measuring a cage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if the machine is old enough that filters are no longer available, that is a genuine reason to replace it. A shop vac you cannot filter properly is a machine that damages itself and fills the room with dust, which is a poor bargain however well the motor still runs. Our <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac guide</a> covers current models with good filtration support.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the Foam Sleeve as a Dry Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The foam sleeve is a splash barrier for wet pickup, not a dust filter. Used on dry debris it captures very little, so the fine fraction goes straight through to the motor and out of the exhaust — the full cost of running filterless, with the appearance of being filtered.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the Filter to Get More Suction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clean cartridge costs a small amount of airflow; a blinded one costs half. The fix for a clogged filter is a collection bag and a tap-out between sessions, not an empty machine. Filterless running trades a few percent of airflow today for motor life and air quality.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Coarse Debris Is Filter-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Leaves carry soil, offcuts carry sawdust, and gravel carries stone dust. There is very little real-world debris genuinely free of a fine fraction, and the filter costs nothing on coarse material anyway. Leave it in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a Perished Filter Gasket</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hardened or torn gasket lets air and dust bypass the cartridge entirely. The machine sounds normal, the filter looks fitted, and the motor is ingesting dust exactly as if there were no filter at all. Check the gasket whenever you clean the cartridge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Keeping a Spare Cartridge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The moment you need a clean filter is always mid-job, and a machine with no spare is the machine that gets run bare 'just this once'. Filters cost $12 to $35 — keeping one on the shelf removes the temptation entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a shop vac without a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only for wet pickup, where the pleated cartridge must come out anyway. For any dry debris the filter is essential — without it, fine dust passes through the impeller into the motor windings and is blown back into the room through the exhaust. The machine appears to work fine, which is exactly why the habit is common.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you run a shop vac without a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three things, none of them visible at the time: fine grit erodes the impeller blades, dust packs into the motor bearings and windings where it raises operating temperature and shortens motor life, and the finest particles are exhausted back into the room at head height. Mineral dust does all three quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a filter to vacuum water with a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No — you must remove the pleated cartridge for wet pickup. Fit the foam sleeve if your model uses one; it is a splash barrier that keeps droplets out of the motor housing rather than a filter. Water pickup creates no airborne dust, so there is nothing for a filter to capture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a shop vac bag instead of a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A bag is a coarse pre-filter that catches the bulk of the debris, which is why it extends cartridge life so much, but the finest particles pass through it. With a bag and no cartridge, those particles reach the impeller and the exhaust. Use both together — that is the combination that works.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to vacuum drywall dust without a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on both counts. Drywall dust is fine enough to reach the motor and pack the windings, and fine enough to hang in the air for hours after being exhausted back into the room. Use a fine dust or HEPA cartridge with a collection bag, and expect to clean or replace the filter after a big sanding job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">My filter clogged mid-job — can I just take it out?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Better to tap the dust cake off it against a hard surface outside and refit, which restores most of the airflow in under a minute. The clog is the real problem, and the fix is a collection bag so the pleats stay clean. Keep a spare cartridge on the shelf so the answer is never 'run it bare'.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my shop vac filter is sealing properly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lift the motor head and check the gasket where the cartridge seals against the cage. A missing, hardened or torn gasket lets dust bypass the filter completely, which produces every symptom of running filterless while the machine looks entirely normal. Replacement gaskets are cheap and worth fitting as soon as one hardens.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
